    "page_title": "Backfill historical data",
    "short_description": "Techniques and configurations for loading historical data ranges through Godzilla pipelines.",
    "body": "<h1>Backfill historical data</h1>
<p>Backfilling lets you stream historical data from your storage or database into downstream targets — bootstrapping new destinations, backpopulating historical records, or replaying data after schema changes.</p>

{{ds:component/alert|variant=info|title=Before you begin|body_html=<p>Ensure your source connector supports range queries or timestamp filtering. See <a href="{{cms:item/93eecc21-ad7e-43ae-a9d5-a0301ad65f1d}}">Connecting a data source</a> for connector details.</p>}}

<h2>1. Define a time-bounded source connector</h2>
<p>Configure your source connector with start and end timestamps (or watermarks) to define the historical boundary. This prevents the backfill job from processing live events prematurely.</p>

{{ds:component/code-block|filename=backfill-source.json|language=json|code=&#123;
  \"type\": \"postgres\",
  \"direction\": \"source\",
  \"config\": &#123;
    \"connection_string\": \"postgres://user:secret@db.example.com:5432/production\",
    \"query\": \"SELECT * FROM orders WHERE created_at >= '2025-01-01T00:00:00Z' AND created_at < '2026-01-01T00:00:00Z'\",
    \"timestamp_column\": \"created_at\"
  &#125;
&#125;}}

<h2>2. Set batch size and concurrency controls</h2>
<p>Historical backfills often involve large data volumes. Adjust your pipeline's batch size and concurrency limit to avoid exhausting memory or triggering rate limits on your destination system.</p>

{{ds:component/code-block|filename=pipeline-backfill.json|language=json|code=&#123;
  \"name\": \"orders-backfill-2025\",
  \"source_connector_id\": \"conn_pg_orders_backfill\",
  \"destination_connector_id\": \"conn_snowflake_dw\",
  \"settings\": &#123;
    \"batch_size\": 5000,
    \"concurrency\": 4,
    \"max_retries\": 5
  &#125;
&#125;}}

{{ds:component/alert|variant=warning|title=Destination throughput|body_html=<p>Monitor destination write latency while the backfill runs. If destination response times spike, reduce pipeline concurrency.</p>}}
